"""Foundations module, part 1."""
from _lib import *

write("basics-problem",
 "Choosing a checkbox over a radio button is a logic question, not a matter of taste. Teams that re-debate settled conventions waste hours and ship contradictions developers spot immediately.",
 [("Three controls, three different logics",
   "**Checkbox** — independent options. Zero, one or many. A single checkbox is one yes/no.\n\n**Radio button** — exactly one of two or more mutually exclusive options.\n\n**Toggle / switch** — a binary state that takes effect immediately, like a setting.\n\nEach control signals a different logic. Picking the wrong one is a mismatch between what the interface says and what the system does.",
   cmp("Seen in review", ["Parent switch over two checkboxes", "Exclusive options as checkboxes", "Required email as a disabled switch"],
       "What the logic calls for", ["Two independent checkboxes", "Radio buttons — exactly one", "Plain text: you'll always get this"]),
   q("A user must pick exactly one billing cycle: monthly or yearly. Which control?",
     ["Radio buttons", "Two checkboxes", "A toggle switch", "A checkbox with a help icon explaining the rule"],
     "Radio buttons mean exactly one of several mutually exclusive options.")),
  ("The email preferences review",
   "A single on/off switch was meant to control separate email and SMS checkboxes underneath it. Nobody could say what the switch should show when only one was ticked, or what toggling it would do.\n\nA legally required email appeared as a preference with a **disabled switch** — offering a choice the user doesn't have.\n\nThe team spent close to an hour on questions that already had answers.", None,
   q("A legally required notification is shown as a greyed-out toggle. What is wrong with that?",
     ["It presents a choice the user doesn't have", "Greyed-out controls fail colour contrast rules", "Toggles should never be used for notifications", "Legal notifications belong on a separate settings page entirely"],
     "If there is no choice, say so in plain text instead of showing a disabled control.")),
  ("Raise the floor, not just the ceiling",
   "The AI-era argument that designers bring “taste” usually means aesthetics. Calabro's order of work:\n\n**user goals → real system states → real user choices → the pattern that fits → then the visual polish**\n\nRe-debating settled conventions teaches the rest of the company that UX is common sense anyone can do.", None,
   q("Where does visual polish come in Calabro's order of work?",
     ["Last, after the pattern is chosen", "First, to get buy-in from stakeholders", "Alongside choosing the control", "It isn't part of a designer's job at all"],
     "Goals, states and choices decide the pattern; polish comes after."))],
 [q("Why does Calabro call control choice a logic question?",
    ["The control has to match what the system actually does", "Logic puzzles are a good interview exercise for designers", "Because engineers prefer logical arguments to visual ones", "Because usability testing can't resolve questions of taste"],
    "A mismatch between control and system state is an error, not a preference."),
  q("What does a single checkbox represent?",
    ["One yes/no choice", "One of several exclusive options", "A setting that applies immediately", "A required field that cannot be skipped"],
    "Checkboxes are independent: zero, one or many; a single one is a yes/no."),
  q("What is the cost of re-debating settled conventions, beyond the lost hours?",
    ["It teaches the company that UX is just common sense", "It delays the visual design phase of the project", "It makes the design system harder to maintain", "It leaves junior designers without clear mentorship"],
    "It devalues the discipline and ships contradictions developers spot immediately."),
  q("Which control is right for a setting that takes effect the moment it changes?",
    ["A toggle switch", "A radio button", "A checkbox", "A dropdown with Apply and Cancel buttons"],
    "A toggle is a binary state that takes effect immediately."),
  q("What does “raise the floor” mean in this article?",
    ["Get the settled basics right everywhere", "Set a higher bar for visual craft", "Hire more senior designers per team", "Move UX earlier in the delivery process"],
    "The problem isn't the ceiling of ambition; it's basics that keep being got wrong.")],
 summary="Control choice is logic, not taste — and re-debating settled conventions has a cost.")

write("id-basics",
 "Academic definitions of interaction design laws are technically correct and practically useless in a design review. Calabro reframes eight of them in plain language.",
 [("Six laws, reframed",
   "**Fitts's Law** — put things near the user's cursor; make the important things bigger.\n**Law of Magic Pixels** — users look in corners and can reach them easily. Use corners for global elements.\n**Hick's Law** — break things into categories and steps when they get complicated.\n**Progressive Disclosure** — show users what they need, when they need it.\n**Miller's Law** — don't make users remember things or think too hard.\n**Affordances & Signifiers** — make it look like what it does; tell users what will happen.",
   cmp("Academic version", ["Time to acquire a target ∝ distance ÷ size", "Decision time increases with the number of choices", "7 ± 2 chunks in working memory"],
       "Plain reframe", ["Put it near the cursor; make it bigger", "Break it into categories and steps", "Don't make users remember or think hard"]),
   q("Which is the plain reframe of Miller's Law that Calabro teaches?",
     ["Don't make users remember things", "Keep every menu to seven items", "Show seven options, then paginate", "Group related options into chunks of five to nine"],
     "He explicitly says it is not about the magic number seven.")),
  ("The two that seem to contradict",
   "Fitts's Law says put things near the predicted cursor. The Law of Magic Pixels says users head for the corners. Which wins?\n\n**Context decides.** Use corners for global elements — navigation, logout, settings. Place contextual elements near the thing being acted on.", None,
   q("Where does a global “Log out” control belong, by these two laws?",
     ["In a corner", "Next to the user's current cursor position", "Centred at the bottom of the page", "Wherever the design system happens to place it"],
     "Corners for global elements; near the cursor for contextual ones.")),
  ("Jakob's Law overrides the rest",
   "Jakob's Law — users spend more time on other sites than yours, so leverage familiar patterns — is the **guiding principle that overlays all the others**.\n\nWhen in doubt, default to what users already expect.\n\nWhy reframe at all? Academic language starts a theoretical discussion. Plain language produces a design decision.", None,
   q("Two laws point to different solutions and the team is stuck. What does Calabro suggest?",
     ["Default to the familiar pattern (Jakob's Law)", "Apply whichever law was published earliest", "Run an A/B test on both versions", "Escalate the decision to the product manager"],
     "He calls Jakob's Law the overriding principle."))],
 [q("Why does Calabro reframe the laws in plain language?",
    ["Plain language produces design decisions", "Academic definitions are factually wrong", "Junior designers don't read research papers", "Plain language is required by most design systems"],
    "Academic phrasing activates theoretical discussion instead."),
  q("What does the Law of Magic Pixels describe?",
    ["Users look for things in corners and reach them easily", "Screens have a fixed number of usable pixels", "Users notice movement before they notice colour", "Magic numbers in layout grids improve scanning"],
    "Hence corners for global elements."),
  q("Which law is the reframe “show users what they need when they need it”?",
    ["Progressive disclosure", "Hick's Law", "Fitts's Law", "The aesthetic-usability effect"],
    "Just-in-time information."),
  q("Hick's Law, in Calabro's reframe, tells you to:",
    ["Break complicated things into categories and steps", "Remove at least half of the options", "Limit menus to a maximum of seven items", "Put the most popular option first in every list"],
    "It is about reducing decision load, not deleting content."),
  q("What is the difference between an affordance and a signifier?",
    ["It looks like what it does vs. it says what will happen", "One is visual and one is written in the code", "One applies to desktop and one to mobile devices", "Affordances are tested; signifiers are decided by the brand"],
    "Affordance: can I interact? Signifier: what happens if I do?")],
 summary="Eight interaction design laws in plain language that actually change design decisions.")

write("best-practice-default",
 "Re-testing established best practices on every project wastes time and puts the burden of proof on the wrong person. Make the rule explicit.",
 [("Flip the burden of proof",
   "The common pattern: the researcher has to prove that icons need labels, that a scale works, that the convention applies here.\n\n**Calabro's rule:** whoever wants to deviate from a well-evidenced practice brings the evidence.\n\nAgreed once at a large tech company, the rule ended months of relitigating and improved outcomes across teams.",
   cmp("Inverted (common)", ["Researcher must prove icons need labels", "Re-test 3-point scales every survey", "“Our users are tech-savvy”"],
       "Fair (Calabro's rule)", ["Challenger brings data to skip labels", "Use existing guidance and move on", "Show the behaviour, not the belief"]),
   q("A stakeholder wants to ship icon-only navigation. Under Calabro's rule, who brings evidence?",
     ["The stakeholder proposing the deviation", "The researcher who raised the concern", "Whichever side has more stakeholders", "Nobody — it should go straight to an A/B test"],
     "The default is the evidenced practice; the exception has to prove itself.")),
  ("Use your head about which research applies",
   "In the 1990s, studies rated colour pairs **on swatches, out of context**. Sites adopted the “winning” palettes and many were unreadable.\n\nA study of icon styles in isolation said little about icons in a real interface.\n\nSome questions need not be asked. Some things need not be tested. And some findings don't transfer.", None,
   q("A study rates colour pairs on isolated swatches. What can you conclude for your product page?",
     ["Very little — the context is missing", "That the winning pair will be most readable", "That colour has no effect on readability", "That the study should be repeated with more participants"],
     "Context-free ratings didn't predict what worked on real pages.")),
  ("Before you cite a study",
   "Check that it studied **your situation**: real interfaces, comparable users, comparable tasks.\n\nThe same discipline that protects you from re-testing the obvious also protects you from citing research that doesn't transfer.", None,
   q("What should you check before citing a study in a design argument?",
     ["Whether it studied a situation like yours", "Whether it was published in the last two years", "Whether the sample was at least 30 people", "Whether its authors are well known in the industry"],
     "Comparable interface, users and tasks — otherwise the finding doesn't transfer."))],
 [q("What is the default, in Calabro's rule?",
    ["The well-evidenced practice", "Whatever the design system specifies", "The option that tests fastest", "The approach the previous team used"],
    "Default to the evidence; make the exception prove itself."),
  q("What did the explicit rule achieve at the large tech company?",
    ["It ended months of relitigating the same debates", "It reduced the research budget by a third", "It replaced the need for a design system", "It let the team ship without any usability testing"],
    "And it improved outcomes across teams."),
  q("Why were the 1990s colour studies misleading?",
    ["Pairs were rated out of context, on swatches", "The samples were too small to be meaningful", "Monitors of the era showed colour inaccurately", "Participants were all design professionals"],
    "Sites adopted the winning palettes and many were unreadable."),
  q("“Our users are tech-savvy” is offered as a reason to skip labels. What does the rule ask for?",
    ["The behaviour, not the belief", "A second opinion from another stakeholder", "A written exception in the design system", "A longer research timeline before deciding"],
    "The claim needs evidence from those users."),
  q("Which is NOT a conclusion of this article?",
    ["Every best practice should be re-tested per project", "Some questions need not be asked", "Some findings don't transfer to your context", "The challenger should bring the evidence"],
    "Re-testing everything is exactly the waste it argues against.")],
 summary="Default to the evidenced practice; whoever wants the exception brings the data.")

write("think-researcher",
 "Researchers are trained to let good evidence change their minds. Calabro wants the same from designers — and holds himself to it.",
 [("“But Google does it” isn't evidence",
   "Google's products are free, pervasive, and can tolerate usability problems most businesses can't. They may succeed **despite** some of their interface choices, not because of them.\n\nCopying a pattern from a dominant product imports the pattern without the market position that absorbs its cost.", None,
   q("A stakeholder justifies a pattern with “Google does it.” What is the flaw?",
     ["Google can absorb usability costs others can't", "Google's designs are legally protected", "Google tests patterns far less than most firms", "Google users are more technical than average users"],
     "Dominance tolerates friction that would cost a smaller business real money.")),
  ("Patterns with decades of evidence against them",
   "Still presented as good ideas:\n\n• Unlabeled icons\n• Split buttons\n• Placeholder text used as a label\n• Floating help and chatbot bubbles\n• Info icons hiding key information\n• Decorative animation\n\nCalabro offers the list as something to send to a designer who genuinely wants to learn — a starting point, not a rulebook.",
   cmp("Still proposed", ["Unlabeled icons", "Split buttons", "Placeholder as label"],
       "Where the evidence is", ["Every Icon Needs a Label", "The Problem With Split Buttons", "Anatomy of an Input Field"]),
   q("How does Calabro describe his list of evidence-backed articles?",
     ["A starting point, not a rulebook", "The definitive standard for interaction design", "A checklist to apply before every release", "A replacement for usability testing"],
     "It is for a designer who genuinely wants to learn.")),
  ("The standard cuts both ways",
   "Don't change your mind because someone says a pattern is bad. **Ask for the evidence, check its method, decide whether it applies to your users.**\n\nAnd if good research with your actual users shows a “bad” pattern works in your context, the researcher has to update too.\n\nExperience should improve your first judgments, not make them untouchable.\n\nThe prompt for the article was a book that changed how Calabro thinks about contextual inquiry — enough that he started piloting the new approach.", None,
   q("Your research shows a widely criticised pattern works well for your users. What should you do?",
     ["Update your position — the evidence cuts both ways", "Dismiss the result as a sampling artefact", "Keep the pattern but don't publish the finding", "Run the study again until it matches the literature"],
     "The same standard applies to researchers."))],
 [q("What does Calabro say experience should do?",
    ["Improve your first judgments without making them untouchable", "Replace the need to check new evidence", "Let you decide design questions faster than juniors", "Qualify you to set standards for the whole team"],
    "Good evidence should still be able to change your mind."),
  q("What should you do when someone tells you a pattern is bad?",
    ["Ask for the evidence and check its method", "Accept it if they are more senior than you", "Change the design and move on quickly", "Put it to a vote in the next design review"],
    "Then decide whether the evidence applies to your users."),
  q("Which is on Calabro's list of patterns with evidence against them?",
    ["Info icons that hide key information", "Breadcrumb navigation on deep pages", "Inline validation on long forms", "Persistent labels above input fields"],
    "The last two are things he recommends."),
  q("Why did a book prompt this article?",
    ["Its approach changed how he thinks about contextual inquiry", "It disproved several of his earlier articles", "It is the only research book he recommends", "It was written by a former colleague of his"],
    "He was piloting the method as a result — learning doing what it should."),
  q("What is the article's view of Google's usability problems?",
    ["Its products may succeed despite them", "They are deliberate experiments on users", "They prove usability rules no longer apply", "They only affect users outside the United States"],
    "Free and pervasive products tolerate friction others cannot.")],
 summary="Let good evidence change your mind — and remember that “Google does it” isn't evidence.")

write("jakobs-law",
 "Jakob's Law is about **prevalence** — patterns users meet constantly across the tools they actually use. One screenshot from a niche product is not evidence of a shared mental model.",
 [("What the law actually says",
   "Users spend most of their time on other sites, so they expect your site to work the way those sites work.\n\nAny convention used on the **majority of products users interact with** gets burned into their mental models, and deviating from it creates friction.\n\nIn practical terms: don't reinvent the wheel.",
   cmp("Invalid use ✗", ["“One analytics tool does it”", "A niche example users rarely meet", "1 screenshot ≠ familiarity"],
       "Valid use ✓", ["“A hamburger opens a mobile menu”", "Met daily across hundreds of apps", "Prevalence = mental model"]),
   q("What makes an appeal to Jakob's Law valid?",
     ["Users meet the pattern constantly, across many tools", "A respected product uses the pattern", "The pattern appears in a major design system", "The team has used the pattern before without complaints"],
     "The law is about prevalence across the user's whole digital environment.")),
  ("The misuse pattern",
   "**1.** A team introduces a novel interaction.\n**2.** A usability concern is raised.\n**3.** Someone finds a single screenshot from a niche tool doing something similar.\n**4.** Jakob's Law is invoked and the discussion ends.\n\nWhat's missing: any serious look at how often users actually encounter that pattern.\n\nOne team hid controls in a dense data table to look “cleaner”. When users couldn't find them in testing, a screenshot from one specialised tool was presented as proof.", None,
   q("Users fail to find hidden table controls, and a screenshot from one niche tool is offered as Jakob's Law proof. What is missing?",
     ["Evidence that users encounter that pattern often", "A larger sample in the usability test", "A second screenshot from another product", "Agreement from the design system team"],
     "Prevalence is the whole point of the law; one precedent isn't prevalence.")),
  ("The test before you invoke it",
   "Calabro's bar: collect examples from **at least five products** that solve the same kind of problem for similar users.\n\nIf you are working on a finance app, that means other finance apps your users have — not a design showcase.\n\nIf you can't do that simply, you are not ready to claim that a familiar pattern exists.", None,
   q("How many comparable products does Calabro ask you to find before claiming a pattern is familiar?",
     ["At least five", "At least two", "At least ten", "One, if it is a market leader"],
     "Five products solving the same problem for similar users."))],
 [q("What is Jakob's Law really about?",
    ["Prevalence across the tools users actually use", "Consistency within a single product", "The speed at which users learn new patterns", "Matching the conventions of your closest competitor"],
    "Not a single precedent."),
  q("Which is a valid example of the law?",
    ["A hamburger icon opening a mobile menu", "A custom gesture used in one analytics tool", "A novel table interaction that tested poorly", "A pattern taken from an award-winning portfolio site"],
    "Users meet the hamburger daily across hundreds of apps."),
  q("Why should finance app examples come from other finance apps?",
    ["They are the tools those users actually spend time in", "Finance apps have stricter regulatory requirements", "Other industries use incompatible design systems", "Finance users dislike patterns from consumer apps"],
    "Prevalence is measured in the user's own environment."),
  q("What is the effect of invoking the law with one screenshot?",
    ["It ends the discussion without evidence", "It satisfies the five-product requirement", "It proves the pattern is a usability risk", "It shifts the decision to the research team"],
    "That is the weaponising the title refers to."),
  q("If you can't easily collect comparable examples, what follows?",
    ["You aren't ready to claim the pattern is familiar", "You should test the pattern with five users", "You should adopt your competitor's version", "The pattern is automatically a bad idea"],
    "No prevalence, no claim — the pattern may still be fine, but not on these grounds.")],
 summary="The law is about prevalence; find five comparable products before claiming users know a pattern.")
print("foundations part 1 written")
